WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.380
Foundations. Anatomy of a stack.

2
00:00:09.398 --> 00:00:12.168
What is actually running when
someone says “the stack”?

3
00:00:13.758 --> 00:00:15.688
A stack is a resource group:

4
00:00:15.758 --> 00:00:19.528
AKS, plus the Azure data
services around it.

5
00:00:19.838 --> 00:00:21.118
Not the cluster alone.

6
00:00:21.118 --> 00:00:26.328
spi up creates AKS and its
Azure resources together.

7
00:00:28.506 --> 00:00:31.076
Some of those resources
belong to a partition,

8
00:00:31.146 --> 00:00:32.596
and some are shared.

9
00:00:32.746 --> 00:00:36.026
For the example partition,
opendes, records,

10
00:00:36.026 --> 00:00:39.716
blobs, and events each have
their own Azure resource:

11
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:45.076
a Cosmos DB account, a Storage
account, and a Service Bus namespace.

12
00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:49.716
Entitlements, identities, and Key
Vault are shared by the environment.

13
00:00:52.146 --> 00:00:56.066
Inside AKS, the OSDU services
run in one namespace,

14
00:00:56.066 --> 00:01:00.426
one image each. Every request reaches
them through the Istio gateway,

15
00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:03.596
which routes each API path to its service.

16
00:01:05.842 --> 00:01:07.212
Follow one request.

17
00:01:07.442 --> 00:01:12.482
A client asks for the opendes partition,
with a bearer token and a data partition

18
00:01:12.482 --> 00:01:15.762
id. The gateway routes it
to the Partition service,

19
00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:19.452
which identifies the caller
and reads stored configuration:

20
00:01:19.602 --> 00:01:21.452
tables in common Storage.

21
00:01:21.602 --> 00:01:25.092
This lookup never touches
the partition's Cosmos DB,

22
00:01:25.362 --> 00:01:28.252
blob Storage, or Service Bus.

23
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.390
An easy mistake:

24
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.880
the stack is just AKS.

25
00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:39.790
AKS runs the Kubernetes workloads, but
the same resource group holds Azure data

26
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.960
services outside the cluster.

27
00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:45.240
To check, list the resources in the group.

28
00:01:47.186 --> 00:01:48.396
So now you can say:

29
00:01:48.626 --> 00:01:53.596
a stack is one resource group, with
partition and shared resources,

30
00:01:53.626 --> 00:01:56.396
and one gateway in front of its services.

31
00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:02.364
Next: where does the Azure
code live in a service?

